A||||12||Rex||cradle interchange||||1||
Z||000000||Rex||06-18-2001||08:15 PM||||Just wondering if cradles are interchangable?<BR>I am wanting to aquire one to sandblast and paint to get ready for a transplant V-6 to V-8 My car is a 86 se would other years fit and would it matter if it was a duke cradle?||||reg||1
Z||000001||cadaver||06-18-2001||09:44 PM||||from what I know 88's are one year only of course. But I think the 84 is also one year only. And the 85-86-87 are all the same.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Galen||06-18-2001||10:03 PM||||I know a 87 will fit a 87  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> dunno about others<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/pictures/angryboy.gif">      <BR>  <A HREF="mailto:galen@spinrpg.com">galen@spinrpg.com</A>   -<A HREF="http://myfiero.spinrpg.com/" TARGET=_blank>1988 Hawaiin Orchid Coupe w/ sunroof</A><BR>AIM: GalenRPG - <A HREF="http://www.webjolt.f2s.com/" TARGET=_blank>WebJolt Pictures</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Songman||06-18-2001||10:45 PM||||Not sure about year to year, but I think the difference between Duke to V6 is a matter of engine and tranny mounts. This probably won't matter if you are going to a V8 anyway. Just weld on your new mounts and you are ready to go.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/songman_1.gif"> <BR><A HREF="http://members.home.com/tnfieros" TARGET=_blank>Tennessee Fiero Owners</A>||||reg||1
Z||000004||bad911||06-18-2001||11:37 PM||||Looks like there were three cradles thru 87... my microfiche doesn't go to 88.<P>10033671 -- 84 - all<BR>            85 - all ex. F41,WS6<BR>            86 - 2.5 MT2 ex. F41<BR>            86 - 87  2.8<BR>            87 - 2.5 w/ MD9, F41<P>10033670 -- 85 - F41, WS6<BR>            86 - 2.5 MT2 w/ F41<P>10039428 -- 86 - 2.5 w/ MD9<P>F41 - HD Susp<BR>WS6 - Perf. pkg.<BR>MT2 - Isuzu 5-spd<BR>MD9 - Auto trans<P>Hope this helps. Thanks to your question I've  delightfully discovered that my 85 has the F41 option. If you're interested, in daylight I'll take a crack at comparing what should be the 670 with the 671.<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>87 2.8 AT SE<BR>85 WannaCaddie<BR>84 Rolling Stock||||reg||1
Z||000005||Kelvin Vivian||06-19-2001||03:38 AM||||Similarly, I too was wondering if an 88 4-cyl cradle can be swapped for an 88 V6 cradle. My next door neighbor is transplanting all of his 1988 GT SCCA racing hardware into a 1988 coupe. The cradles look very similar with the exception that the 4-cyl cradle has little parts welded onto it instead of bolted. We tried to look for part numbers, but corresponding numbers were different. The current 88 V6 cradle is warped a 1/8 of an inch...||||reg||5
Z||000006||Will||06-19-2001||08:20 AM||||AFAIK, the mounting point measurements are all the same. All cradles should be interchangeable in that respect. V6 vs I4 is only a matter of which holes you use. All the cradles I've seen have mounting holes for both.<P>The part number differences are likely very small things that you could only see if you put the bare cradles right next to eachother.||||reg||1
Z||000007||TONY_C||06-19-2001||08:35 AM||||The 84 thru 87 cradles can be interchanged with little difficulty. the most important difference is the engine mounts for the 4 vs. 6 engines. the 88 cradle is different. First off, it doesnt use bushings, it bolts hard to the chassis and althouigh the front bolts fit easily, the rear bolt holes on the subframe need to be slotted. the other major issue with the 88 cradle is that the struts do not mount in the same location as the 84-87 struts. They dont even have the same bolt circle mounting pattern. the struts are located further in and toward the front on 88's. hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Will||06-19-2001||03:39 PM||||If the rear cradle mounting holes on an '88 cradle need to be slotted to go into your car, then either your chassis is bent or your cradle is bent.<BR>The specifications in the shop manuals call out the mounting point dimensions as being the same, down to the millimeter.<P>The strut problem can be overcome by slotting the camber asjustment holes in an early strut more than they already are. This will allow the camber to be brought into spec. It won't be <I>exactly</I> like an '88, but it will be better than the early rear suspension.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Toddster||06-19-2001||04:08 PM||||Tony_C and Will are right. The 88 cradle is an relatively easy swap and WELL worth the effort.<P>I have a spare I'd be happy to sell ya cheap.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Rex||06-19-2001||11:09 PM||||Thanks Toddster,the freight would probley kill though.||||reg||1
Z||000011||bad911||06-21-2001||02:05 AM||||Well, the 10033671/670 dif is major/minor... your perspective. Instead of tranny mounts fore and aft, there's a single mount on the left end ("rear") of the trans, cradle-left. The cradle difference is a unique piece welded atop the left member, ahead of the axle and above the front leg of the A-arm. (Gonna play _REAL_ nicely into an AT Caddie conversion). Here's the mount on the '85 w/F41, looking into the wheelwell over the left caliper ('scuze the mess, not de-abused yet). And you guys are right, all the other mount holes are there. The logic of this F41's mounting arrangement's "performance" aspect escapes me. WTF, over? And geez, you '88 guys are _killing_ us.  Can aluminum bushings come close? (I knew those '87 fiches were too cheap, dammit...)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/mountpad.jpg"> <BR> <P>------------------<BR>87 2.8 AT SE<BR>85 Caddie conv. in process<BR>84 2M4||||reg||1
Z||000012||FieroMaster88||06-21-2001||02:31 AM||||So would i be able to take a V6 and a 2.8 from an 85 GT and swap it into my car no problem???  Cradle and all?  Or will the engine mount right to my 88 cradle??<P>Just wondering<P>------------------<BR>        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/FieroMaster88.gif">        <BR>James Essar<BR>AOL IM Name: FieroMaster88<BR>88 Coupe 30,000 miles  <IMG SRC="http://cwm.ragesofsanity.com/s/net4/smiling2.gif"> ||||reg||5
